// A debugger's view of tagged unions (bin/run-tests runs it under gdb with
// tagged_unions.gdb and under cdb with tagged_unions.cdb; issue #224,
// spec 10.7): a tagged union shown as the C struct it is laid out as, a
// `tag` naming the member it holds and an anonymous union of its members
// that aren't void, so s.circle is reached as in C, and a switch's `it` a
// pointer to the member in the union itself, so a write through it shows
// in s. Each case's `it` is a variable of its own, of its own member's
// type, in the case's block only: the second switch's `.rect` case shows a
// *Rect, and after the switch there is no `it`. A void member's union
// shows its other members over zeroed bytes (e's key is 0). A member named
// tag is listed as tag$, so k.tag is the tag and k.tag$ the member (a
// second `tag` crashes gdb). gdb writes the tag as its enumerator's name
// (`tag = circle`), and ptype the tag's type as `enum {circle, rect,
// empty}`; cdb's `??` writes it as its number and name, `0 ( circle )`,
// and lists the anonymous union's members in the struct itself, at the
// members' offset (circle and rect at +0x004), as it does any anonymous
// member's; its `dv /t` lists `it`, in the case's block, before main's own
// variables.
putchar :: (c: s32) -> s32 #foreign libc;

Circle :: struct { r: float; }
Rect :: struct { w: float; h: float; }
Shape :: union #tagged { circle: Circle; rect: Rect; empty: void; }
Event :: union #tagged { key: s32; quit: void; }
Token :: union #tagged { word: s32; tag: s32; }

main :: () {
    s := Shape.{circle = .{2}};
    e := Event.quit;
    k := Token.{tag = 5};
    if s == {
        case .circle;
            it.r = 3;
            putchar(10);
        case;
    }
    s = .{rect = .{4, 5}};
    if s == {
        case .rect;
            it.w = 6;
            putchar(10);
        case;
    }
    putchar(10);
}
